  card="The year 1967 ended the era of single-party dominated elections in India and began an era of alliances. In 1977, against the backdrop of the National Emergency, various parties formed the Janata Party alliance, which formed the first coalition government at the national level. Since the late 1990s the National Democratic Alliance (NDA), led by the BJP, and the United Progressive Alliance (UPA), led by the Indian National Congress until its dissolution in 2023 and reconstitution as the Indian National Developmental Inclusive Alliance, have been the major alliances. Conducting elections for over 96.8 crore voters with thousands of polling stations and hundreds of parties across diverse regions is challenging. Challenges include misinformation, fake news and intimidation, which the ECI addresses through the RPA 1950 and 1951, the Model Code of Conduct, EVMs, the Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail (VVPAT), voters' awareness campaigns and other measures. With constant vigilance and active citizen participation elections become more representative and democracy more robust.",
